#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83724b66-0f15-5b94-9576-76e9dfd56d38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Higher-Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7711932b-125e-5d2c-94e1-24e735a181e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360813} {:answer/id #uuid "354a7545-e85e-59df-b65d-d2eefb2ac3ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360814} {:answer/id #uuid "2528ed8a-e6b9-5549-896f-9cc56b700eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360815} {:answer/id #uuid "142d340b-0f5a-5abf-932b-039c8dc51fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360816} {:answer/id #uuid "1bae5314-0108-50bb-97e6-13d0524a8345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "142d340b-0f5a-5abf-932b-039c8dc51fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360816} :answer-field/id #uuid "0008eab6-b6ff-5522-9bc3-18ed3af8ed73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "130ad7f8-2367-56d1-afed-1995cee8fbfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132984" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{z\\to1,\\ z\\ne1}f_{xyz}(-1,2,z)$ for $f(x,y,z)=x^3y^2z^2-\\frac{3}{\\ln z}$, defined on $z>0$, $z\\ne1$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{xy}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{x}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{xyz}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{xy}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyz}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({f}_{xy}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(6{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate ${f}_{xyz}(x,y,z)$ at $(-1,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyz}(-1,2,1) & =12(-1)^{2}(2)(1)=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDomain clarification: the original function is undefined at $z=1$, since $\\ln1=0$. Consequently the literal value $f_{xyz}(-1,2,1)$ in the source prompt is undefined. The derivatives above are valid on $z>0$, $z\\ne1$; there $f_x=3x^2y^2z^2$, $f_{xy}=6x^2yz^2$, and $f_{xyz}=12x^2yz$. Thus $f_{xyz}(-1,2,z)=24z$ and\n$$\\lim_{z\\to1,\\ z\\ne1}f_{xyz}(-1,2,z)=24.$$\nThe clarified study question explicitly asks for this limit, or continuous extension of the derivative, so the source's accepted answer $24$ and all original choices remain valid. This extension does not define the original function or its literal derivative at the excluded point."}]}
